  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe proposed method uses a convex optimization framework to minimize the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. By treating each shift as a linear constraint, we transform the non-convex problem into a set of linear inequalities, enabling the use of efficient convex solvers. The approach leverages CVXPY to solve the resulting semi-infinite linear program, iteratively refining the constraints to find a near-optimal solution within the given budget.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nWe aim to minimize C subject to:\n\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. sum(h) = n_points / 2\n3. For each shift k \u2208 [-n_points+1, n_points-1]:\n   sum_{i=0}^{n_points - |k| - 1} h[i] * (1 - h[i + sign(k)*|k|]) \u2264 C / dx\n\nwhere dx = 2.0 / n_points.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialize variables\n   - n_points = len(initial_h_values)\n   - h = initial_h_values.copy()\n   - dx = 2.0 / n_points\n   - C = evaluate_erdos_solution(h)[0]\n\nb. Formulate optimization problem\n   - Define variables: h (n_points) and C\n   - Create constraints for each shift k\n\nc. Solve iteratively\n   - Use CVXPY to solve the convex program\n   - Update h and C until C is minimized or budget exhausted\n\nd. Validate and return\n   - Ensure h meets constraints\n   - Return (h, C, n_points)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- Use initial_h_values as a starting point\n- Adjust solver parameters for stability\n- Limit shifts considered to reduce complexity\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n- Time per iteration: ~10s for large n\n- Maximum iterations: 100 within budget\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n- Verify constraints\n- Confirm C is evaluated correctly\n- Return feasible h with minimized C",
